SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING - THE WEEKEND READ
Saturday, March 7, 2026

Freight brokerages fail quietly. There is no bankruptcy filing for most of them; the phones stop being answered, the carriers stop being paid, and a few weeks later the factoring company sends a letter. By our count 210 brokerages with more than $5M in annual revenue have closed in the last twelve months, about 40% more than the year before, and the pace is picking up rather than slowing.

The easy explanation is the freight recession: two and a half years of falling rates squeezed the spread brokers live on. That is true and incomplete. The brokers failing now are not the small ones. They are mid-size firms that grew fast in 2021, hired sales teams on the assumption that spreads stay wide, and built their operations on people rather than software. When rates fell, their cost per load stayed where it was.

The survivors have a different shape. They run fewer people per load, price with algorithms rather than gut, and have quietly become software companies that happen to move freight. Two of the largest now make more gross profit from their carrier-facing tools than from brokerage itself. That is the shape of the industry in five years, and it is worth noticing that it happened through attrition rather than through any one firm winning.

A practical note for shippers: check your brokers' payment terms to carriers. A broker stretching carriers to 60 days is a broker three months from a phone that does not answer.

